ow:auto;display:block;color:#212529;overflow-wrap:normal;word-break:normal=
;padding:.0px;">Hi Paul:

I tried and look up the documents GMT.0009-2012 and GBT.32918.5-2016 on=20=

the (non-secured) websites you referenced, but only found Chinese=20
versions (and Chinese website navigation panels [pardon my poor language=20=

skills here]). Since the ISO documents are not available to the general=20=

public without payment, it would be helpful to have a freely available=20=

document (in English) from an authoritative source. Having such a=20
reference available would be helpful to the IETF community (and=20
researchers). Please note that BSI provides its specifications in German=20=

and English, so as to foster use/study by the community. If the Chinese=20=

national algorithms would be available in similar form, this would serve=20=

a similar purpose.

FYI - I am interested in full details and some time last year I tried to=20=

download specs, but only Parts 2, 4, and 5 were available [1], [2], [3],=20=

not Parts 1 and 3.

Best regards, Rene

[1] China ECC - Public Key Cryptographic Algorithm SM2 Based on ECC -=20
Part 5 - Parameter Definition (SEMB, July 24, 2018)
[2] China ECC - Public Key Cryptographic Algorithm SM2 Based on ECC -=20
Part 2 - Digital Signature Algorithm (SEMB, July 24, 2018)
[3] China ECC - Public Key Cryptographic Algorithm SM2 Based on ECC -=20
Part 4 - Public Key Encryption Algorithm (SEMB, July 24, 2018)

On 8/15/2019 10:16 AM, Paul Yang wrote:
&gt; Hi all,
&gt;
&gt; I have submitted a new internet draft to introduce the SM cipher=20
&gt; suites into TLS 1.3 protocol.
&gt;
&gt; <a =
href=3D"https://tools.ietf.org/html/draft-yang-tls-tls13-sm-suites-00" =
rel=3D"noopener" target=3D"_blank" =
style=3D"outline:none;text-decoration:none;color:#337ab7;box-sizing:border=
-box;background-color:transparent;" moz-do-not-send=3D"true" =
class=3D"">https://tools.ietf.org/html/draft-yang-tls-tls13-sm-suites-00</=
a>
&gt;
&gt; SM cryptographic algorithms are originally a set of Chinese =
national=20
&gt; algorithms and now have been (or being) accepted by ISO as=20
&gt; international standards, including SM2 signature algorithm, SM3 =
hash=20
&gt; function and SM4 block cipher. These algorithms have already been=20=

&gt; supported some time ago by several widely used open source=20
&gt; cryptographic libraries including OpenSSL, BouncyCastle, Botan, =
etc.
&gt;
&gt; Considering TLS1.3 is being gradually adopted in China's internet=20=

&gt; industry, it's important to have a normative definition on how to =
use=20
&gt; the SM algorithms with TLS1.3, especially for the mobile internet=20=

&gt; scenario. Ant Financial is the company who develops the market =
leading=20
&gt; mobile app 'Alipay' and supports payment services for Alibaba=20
&gt; e-commerce business. We highly are depending on the new TLS1.3=20
&gt; protocol for both performance and security purposes. We expect to =
have=20
&gt; more deployment of TLS1.3 capable applications in China's internet=20=

&gt; industry by this standardization attempts.
&gt;
&gt; It's very appreciated to have comments from the IETF TLS list :-)
&gt;
&gt; Many thanks!
&gt;
&gt; _______________________________________________
